  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A P-channel part: drain voltage, gate drive and drain current are all specified as negative values. Gate threshold is -0.40 V to -1.2 V (-0.55 V typ) at -250 µA with VDS = VGS. On-resistance is specified at two drive levels: 0.065 Ω max at -4.5 V and -3.7 A, and 0.135 Ω max at -2.5 V and -3.1 A. Driving the gate to -5.0 V with -10 V and -3.7 A at the drain takes 8.0 nC typ, 2.8 nC of it Miller charge; in Fig 6 the plateau sits near -1.7 to -2.0 V. In Fig 12 on-resistance at -3.7 A falls from about 0.12 Ω at -2.25 V on the gate to about 0.04 Ω at -7 V. In Fig 13 on-resistance at -2.5 V drive rises steeply above about -5 A, passing 0.2 Ω near -9.3 A, while at -4.5 V it stays between about 0.047 Ω and 0.056 Ω from -2 A to -30 A."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "With VDD = -10 V, -3.7 A, RG = 89 Ω and RD = 2.7 Ω, turn-on delay is 350 ns, rise 48 ns, turn-off delay 588 ns and fall 381 ns, all typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Current and power ratings are referred to ambient. Mounted on one square inch of single-layer 1 oz copper, junction-to-ambient resistance is 75 °C/W typ and 100 °C/W max, giving 1.3 W at 25 °C and 0.8 W at 70 °C with 0.01 W/°C derating. Continuous drain current is -3.7 A at 25 °C and -2.2 A at 70 °C with -4.5 V drive; Fig 9 takes the limit to zero at a case temperature of 150 °C. On-resistance at -4.5 V and -3.7 A is about 0.77 times its 25 °C value at -55 °C and about 1.42 times at 150 °C (Fig 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Safe operating area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Fig 8 gives single-pulse limits at TC = 25 °C with the junction at 150 °C, for 10 µs, 100 µs, 1 ms and 10 ms pulses, bounded by on-resistance at low drain voltage, by -22 A and by -20 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Avalanche",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Single-pulse avalanche energy is rated 11 mJ, starting from TJ = 25 °C with 1.65 mH, RG = 25 Ω and -3.7 A. Fig 10 shows the allowed energy falling to zero as the starting junction temperature approaches 150 °C, with more allowed at lower peak current (about 24 mJ at -1.7 A from 25 °C)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The integral diode carries -1.3 A continuous and -22 A pulsed, drops at most -1.2 V at -1.0 A, and recovers from -1.0 A at -100 A/µs in 29 ns typ (43 ns max) with 11 nC typ (17 nC max) of charge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package and marking",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The Micro3 is the SOT-23 outline (JEDEC TO-236AB) with an enlarged lead frame pad for heat, and stands under 1.1 mm. The top face carries the part code X (E for the IRLML6402), a year character Y, a work-week letter W and an assembly lot code; marks above and below the code flag lead-free, and copper wire with halogen-free construction. For work weeks 1 to 26 Y is the last digit of the year; for weeks 27 to 52 it is a letter, A for 2001 or 2011 through K for 2010 or 2020. W steps A to Z through each half year. The scheme covers parts built after 02/26/2001. Recommended footprint and soldering are covered in application note AN-994."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Qualification",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Lead-free, RoHS compliant and halogen-free; moisture sensitivity level 1; qualified to the consumer level."
  }
